ADVERSARIAL FORTH COMPILER TEST SUITE
=====================================

These tests are compiler-agnostic. They test standard Forth semantics.
Any compliant Forth compiler must pass all tests.

RUNNING
-------
From a Forth interpreter:
  include run-all.fs

Or run individual test files:
  include 0001-stack-basic.fs

TEST CATEGORIES
---------------
0001 - Basic stack operations (dup, drop, swap, over, rot, nip, tuck)
0002 - Arithmetic edge cases (negative numbers, zero, overflow)
0003 - Comparison operations (=, <>, <, >, 0<, 0=)
0004 - Control flow (if/then/else, begin/until/while, do/loop)
0005 - Return stack operations (>r, r>, r@, 2>r, 2r>)
0006 - Memory operations (!, @, +!, c!, c@, here, ,)
0007 - Logic and bitwise (and, or, xor, invert, lshift, rshift)
0008 - Double-cell operations (s>d, d+, d-, 2dup, 2swap, 2over)
0009 - Mixed-precision arithmetic (*/, um*, m*, etc.)
0010 - Recursion (factorial, fibonacci, mutual recursion)
0011 - String operations (s", compare, /string, move)
0012 - Immediate words (literal, postpone, ['], state)
0013 - CREATE DOES> (the heart of Forth extensibility)
0014 - Exception handling (catch, throw, abort")
0015 - Values (value, to, 2value)
0016 - Deferred words (defer, is, action-of)
0017 - Parsing words (parse-name, word, source)
0018 - Number conversion (base, <#, #, #s, #>, hold, sign)
0019 - Loop control (leave, unloop)
0020 - Case statement (case, of, endof, endcase)

PHILOSOPHY
----------
These tests exist because compilers lie.
They claim compliance but fail on edge cases.
Run these tests. Find the lies.

-- Chuck Moore would say: "If it passes, it might be a Forth.
   If it fails, it definitely is not."
